artial solutions.\n\nThus, no indices should be selected.\n\n<results>\n</results>\n</reasoning>', '<reasoning>\nThe provided partial solution is only one example (i0), and it outlines a logical strategy to solve the problem. It begins by calculating the sum of all numbers (241), which is less than the target (270), indicating that multiplication is necessary. It then factors 270 and identifies 27 × 10 = 270 as a promising path, given that 27 is one of the available numbers. The approach then attempts to form 10 from the remaining numbers (75, 17, 52, 33, 37) through combinations of addition and subtraction.\n\nThis is a valid and reasonable strategy: leveraging known factors of the target and using one number as a multiplier. The method is grounded in number theory and arithmetic reasoning, and it systematically explores plausible pathways. Since only one partial solution is provided (i0), and no other indices (like i1, i2, i3) are present in the input, there are no alternative approaches to compare or prune.\n\nTherefore, the only approach available is i0, and it is logically sound, uses all numbers once (as per the method described), and follows a structured path toward the solution. There are no duplicates or flawed approaches to eliminate, and no other distinct or diverse approaches exist in the set.\n\nGiven the constraints and the absence of multiple partial solutions, i0 is the only valid and unique approach available.\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nThe provided partial solution is only one example (i0), and it outlines a reasoning path toward forming 939 using the numbers 6, 80, 5, 5, 45, 16. The approach involves identifying a near-multiple of the target (e.g., 45 × 20 = 900) or a large multiplication (e.g., 80 × 12 = 960), then attempting to construct the required components (like 20 or 12) from the remaining numbers using basic arithmetic operations.\n\nThe core logic in i0 is to:\n- Recognize that 939 is close to 900 (45 × 20),