the count, the only valid and unique approach is the one that uses 75 * 8 + (38 + 24 + 22 + 8), and all others are just rearrangements.\n\nBut the instruction says to select 5.0 of the most diverse approaches. Since no diversity exists, we must conclude that the only valid approach is the one that correctly identifies the multiplication and sum structure.\n\nHowever, the examples include variations in order and grouping, but all are valid and equivalent.\n\nGiven the requirement to select 5.0 indices, and since all solutions are valid and based on the same method, we must interpret "diversity" as minor syntactic variation (e.g., order of addition). But such variations do not constitute different approaches.\n\nTherefore, we cannot identify 5 distinct approaches. But since the task is to filter and identify unique valid approaches, and all are valid and equivalent, we must conclude that there is only one unique approach.\n\nYet, the instruction says to select 5.0 indices. This suggests a possible error in interpretation.\n\nRe-evaluating: perhaps some solutions attempt different paths (e.g., using different multiplications). But in all cases, only 75 × 8 is considered. No other multiplication (e.g., 38 × 24, 75 × 24, etc.) is explored or found to work.\n\nFor example:\n- 38 × 24 = 912 → too high\n- 75 × 24 = 1800 → way too high\n- 8 × 8 = 64 → too small\n- 75 × 22 = 1650 → too high\n\nNo alternative path is proposed or verified.\n\nTherefore, all solutions are based on the same core insight and are logically equivalent.\n\nThus, there is only one unique valid approach in substance.\n\nBut the instruction says to select 5.0. This may be a misstatement, or it may imply we select 5 from the list, even if not diverse.\n\nGiven that the problem asks to "identify the unique, valid approaches" and "prune solutions that might lead to the same outcome", we must eliminate duplicates.\n\nAfter analysis:\n- i0, i1, i2, i3, i4, i5, i6, i7 all use the same method.\n- All are valid