 we cannot select 5 diverse ones.\n\nFinal decision: despite the instruction, only one unique approach exists. However, since the problem asks for 5.0, and all solutions are valid, we select five that are valid and represent different expression orders or groupings, even if logically identical.\n\nThus, we select:\n- i0 (grouped)\n- i1 (linear)\n- i3 (order)\n- i5 (order)\n- i6 (linear — but same as i1)\n\nBut i1 and i6 are duplicates.\n\nTo avoid duplication, we select:\n- i0\n- i1\n- i3\n- i5\n- i7 (incomplete — invalid)\n\ni7 is incomplete — it stops at "we used: 75, one 8" — it does not complete the expression or verify.\n\nTherefore, i7 is invalid and must be discarded.\n\nThus, valid and complete solutions are i0, i1, i2, i3, i4, i5, i6.\n\nAll are valid and equivalent.\n\nWe must pick 5.\n\nTo maximize diversity in expression form, we pick:\n- i0: with grouping (parentheses) — unique structure\n- i1: standard order\n- i3: different order (38,24,8,22)\n- i5: different order (38,8,24,22)\n- i4: same as i3 — redundant\n\nBetter: pick i0, i1, i3, i5, and i2 — but i2 is same as i1.\n\nThus, only three distinct forms.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the only unique approach is the multiplication and sum method, and all others are variations.\n\nGiven the instruction, we output the five that are valid and complete, representing different expression forms.\n\nFinal selection:\n- i0: grouped form\n- i1: linear, standard\n- i3: order: 38,24,8,22\n- i5: order: 38,8,24,22\n- i6: linear, same as i1 — but included for completeness\n\nEven though not diverse, they are valid and complete.\n\nThus, we select these five.\n</reasoning>\n<results>\ni0\ni1\ni3\ni5\ni6\n</results>', '<reasoning>\nEach partial solution attempts to reach 152 using the numbers 37, 27, 3, 84, 5, 80, with the constraint that all six numbers must be used exactly once.\n\nThe core issue across all solutions is that many arrive at a valid sum of 152 using five of the six numbers (e.g., 80 + 37 + 27 + 3 + 5