rs and operations.\n- It does not provide a working expression; it only explores possibilities without closure.\n- It fails to use all numbers in a valid expression.\n\nThere are no other partial solutions listed beyond i0. Since only one partial solution is provided and it is incomplete and incorrect, there are no valid or diverse approaches among the given partial solutions.\n\nTherefore, based on the instructions, we must identify and select only the *unique, valid approaches*. Since no valid approach is present, and the only one offered is flawed, we conclude that there are no correct partial solutions.\n\nHowever, the instruction says to "select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — implying at least one valid one exists. Given that the provided content only contains one partial solution (i0), and it is not valid, we must infer that either:\n- The partial solution is incomplete or incorrect, or\n- The set of partial solutions is empty or invalid.\n\nSince no valid approach is demonstrated, and the task is to filter out incorrect approaches and identify unique valid ones, and none exist in the input, the correct response is that there are no valid approaches.\n\nBut the output format requires providing selected indices. Given that only one partial solution exists and it is incorrect, there are no valid approaches to select.\n\nThus, under strict interpretation, no valid approach is present — so no indices should be selected.\n\nHowever, the instruction says: "Select 1 of the most diverse approaches" — which implies at least one must be chosen. Given that the only provided approach (i0) is the only one attempted, and despite its flaws, it represents a structured, logical attempt (even if incomplete), and if we interpret "diverse" as the only one present, it may be selected as the only available method.\n\nBut it is incorrect — it fails to form 17 from the numbers, and no expression is built.\n\nTherefore, since no valid approach is present, and the task is to filte